    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A league of mainly German merchant towns, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, that dominated Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> trade from the 14th to the 16th century.</li><li>It had no constitution, army or treasury; diets coordinated policy, and four great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontor">Kontore</a> traded at Novgorod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</li><li>Strong territorial states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> competition and the shift of trade to the Atlantic eroded it; the last diet met in 1669.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1473 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a> privateer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-beneke">Paul Beneke</a> captured a ship carrying Hans Memling’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-last-judgment-memling">Last Judgment</a> triptych; the altarpiece was taken to Danzig and is still in Gdańsk today.</p></aside>
    </section>

