    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion.jpg" alt="Henry the Lion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_the_Lion_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> grew out of the German eastward expansion of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, founded in 1143 by Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-ii-of-holstein">Adolf II of Holstein</a> and refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, in 1159, gave German merchants direct access to the Baltic. They formed a sworn association on Gotland, trading through Visby to Novgorod, and new German towns such as Riga, Reval (Tallinn), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a> (Gdańsk), Rostock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> were founded along the southern and eastern coasts, many of them under Lübeck law. In the West, merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and Westphalian towns had long held privileges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The word <em>Hanse</em> originally meant a company of merchants travelling abroad, and for a long time the Hansa was an association of merchants rather than of cities. Towns increasingly took over the protection of their traders: Lübeck and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> made an alliance in 1241, and the Wendish towns of the southern Baltic coordinated policy from the later thirteenth century. The first general diet, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hansetag">Hansetag</a></em>, met in Lübeck in 1356 to deal with problems at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> office, and historians usually date the “Hansa of the towns” from then; the expression <em>stede van der dudeschen hanse</em> appears in documents soon afterwards.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergen.jpg" alt="Bergen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergen_panorama_at_night_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TomasEE, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hansa linked the raw materials of the Baltic and the North with the manufactures of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. From Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> came <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and wax; from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, pitch, potash and amber; from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron; from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> the dried cod known as stockfish; and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> coast of southern Sweden, then Danish, the herring salted with salt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. Westward these goods went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> in exchange for Flemish and English cloth, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and metal goods; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> also exported large quantities of beer. The cog, a capacious single-masted ship, and later the larger hulk carried this trade. Merchant families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildebrand-veckinchusen">Veckinchusens</a>, whose letters survive, operated through partnerships rather than large firms, and bookkeeping remained simple compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Hanseatic towns were governed by merchant patricians organised in town councils, beneath whom stood craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> that sometimes rebelled, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> in 1408. Middle Low German served as the common language of trade and administration from London to Novgorod. The towns invested their wealth in brick Gothic churches, town halls and warehouses, from the Marienkirche in Lübeck to the wharf at Bergen, many of which survive today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Valdemar IV of Denmark" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waldemar_IV_Otherday_of_Denmark_c_1375_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown contemporary 14th-century artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hansa had no standing army or permanent fleet. When its interests were threatened, member towns agreed at the diet to raise ships and soldiers, usually armed merchantmen hired or requisitioned for a campaign, or to impose a trade embargo, which was often its most effective weapon. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in 1358–1360 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> to restore the merchants’ privileges. The great test came when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a> sacked Visby in 1361 and defeated a Hanseatic fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> in 1362. In 1367 the towns formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-cologne">Confederation of Cologne</a> with Holstein, Mecklenburg and Swedish allies, ravaged the Danish coasts and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stralsund-1370">Peace of Stralsund</a> of 1370 gave them control of the Scanian fortresses for fifteen years and a voice in the Danish succession. The towns then fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victual-brothers">Victual Brothers</a>, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaus-stortebeker">Klaus Störtebeker</a> was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> around 1401. Later wars were less decisive: a conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vordingborg">Peace of Vordingborg</a> in 1435, and a naval war with Holland ended in 1441. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-hanseatic-war">Anglo-Hanseatic War</a> of 1469–1474, fought largely by privateers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-beneke">Paul Beneke</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a>, ended favourably, but the Count’s Feud of 1534–1536 in Denmark was a costly failure for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hansetag"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hansetag.jpg" alt="Hansetag" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hansetag">Hansetag</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haupthandelsroute_Hanse.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flo Beck, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hansa never had a constitution, a common treasury, an official seal or a head, and its membership was never fixed: some seventy towns took an active part, while up to about two hundred were associated with it at its height. Its central institution was the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hansetag">Hansetag</a></em>, the diet of town envoys that met irregularly, usually in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, and issued resolutions known as <em>Rezesse</em>; many towns did not attend, and decisions bound members only in practice. Lübeck, the “head of the Hansa”, presided and handled correspondence. The towns were grouped into regional thirds, and later quarters, led by Lübeck, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a> and other leading cities, which held their own regional meetings. Abroad, the four great offices or <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontor">Kontore</a></em> at Novgorod (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterhof-novgorod">Peterhof</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> (the German Wharf), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steelyard">Steelyard</a>) were self-governing communities with their own aldermen, statutes, treasuries and courts; the Bergen and Novgorod offices imposed strict, almost monastic discipline on young merchants. The ultimate sanction against a disobedient town was exclusion from the Hansa, <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verhansung">Verhansung</a></em>, used against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> in 1285 and Cologne in 1471. The members remained subject to their own lords, from the emperor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, which limited the league’s capacity to act as a state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bryggen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bryggen.jpg" alt="Bryggen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bryggen">Bryggen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bryggen,_Bergen,_Noruega,_2019-09-08,_DD_115-117_PAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For about two centuries the Hansa organised the commerce of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and spread a common urban culture around the Baltic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law, granted to around a hundred towns, shaped municipal government from Holstein to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, and German merchant elites dominated towns such as Riga and Reval until modern times. Brick Gothic churches, town halls and merchant houses gave the region a shared architectural style; the old towns of Lübeck, Visby, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a>, Tallinn and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryggen">Bryggen</a> wharf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. Middle Low German left many loanwords in the Scandinavian languages. The Hansa’s privileges abroad, defended by embargoes rather than armies, also showed how a network of towns without a state could hold its own against kings for a time. Politically it influenced the Scandinavian kingdoms: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stralsund-1370">Peace of Stralsund</a> marked the high point of its power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and Lübeck’s support helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> become king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1523. Its memory remains strong: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> still call themselves Free and Hanseatic Cities, other towns use the H in vehicle registrations, and since 1980 a modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> of cities has revived the name for cultural cooperation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-iii.jpg" alt="Ivan III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_III_of_Russia_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hansa declined slowly from the later fifteenth century as the conditions that had favoured it changed. Territorial states grew stronger and less willing to tolerate foreign privileges: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III of Moscow</a> closed the Novgorod office in 1494, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>’s influence after the Count’s Feud, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a> revoked the privileges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steelyard">London Steelyard</a> in 1598. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants, with larger ships, cheaper freight and a stronger financial base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, took over much of the Baltic grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> trade passing through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Danish Sound</a>, while the herring shoals moved from the Baltic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. The shift of European trade towards the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> reduced the relative importance of the northern routes. The members’ interests also diverged: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a> and the other Prussian towns came under Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1466 and pursued their own policies, and the Livonian towns fell to Sweden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the wars after 1558. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> devastated much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and its trade. A last diet met in Lübeck in 1669, attended by only a handful of towns, and no successor diet was ever summoned. Lübeck, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> continued a looser alliance and kept the Hanseatic name.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> was a loose association of mainly German towns that controlled the trade of the Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> for two centuries, showing how a network of merchant cities without a common state, army or treasury could dominate a region’s commerce and shape its towns.</em></p>

